 :reading
 ["IC02 got SLOWER between the last two runs -- 80.9 to 102.2 ms -- while Neo4j on the same query held steady (5.5 to 5.3 ms), so the host was not the cause for that query. The change intended to fix IC02 made it worse."
  "IC02's regression is real and consistent: 7.7 -> 14.7 -> 19.2 across three runs, monotone. It is not noise."
  "IC08 and IC09 improved again (53.3 -> 38.7, 35.8 -> 21.6), but the skip only fires when groups equal bindings and neither of those steps is in that case, so those movements are not this change either -- Neo4j's own IC09 went 50.7 -> 78.6 ms in the same run, which says the host moved. Single-run p50s over 15 samples do not support attributing 20-30% swings."
  "What IS robust across all three runs is the direction and size of IC09 (85.3 -> 21.6) and IC08 (97.5 -> 38.7), and the direction of IC02 (worse, every time)."]
 :action-taken
 "The skip-the-group-by change is being reverted. It has no demonstrated benefit and the one measurement that speaks to it says it hurts, which is exactly the standard this repo already set for itself when a block cache was measured out of the trampoline path (kotobase-protocols-worker 313e298): an unproven optimisation in a performance-critical path is surface without payment."
 :ic02-still-unexplained
 "The cause of IC02's regression is now UNKNOWN, not merely unfixed. The next step is the one that worked for IC09: instrument the actual query rather than reason about it -- separate probe cost from join cost per clause, print the plan, and find where the time went, instead of proposing a second mechanism."}
